Essay: *El escritor argentino y la tradición*, 1932, repr. 1951 in *Otras inquisiciones / Other Inquisitions*, “The Argentine Writer and the Tradition”. The anti-nationalist parenthesis *las ilusiones del patriotismo no tienen fin / the illusions of patriotism have no end* is the one Borges re-uses from the *Patria / El tamaño de mi esperanza* material in that essay.

In the 1951 revised / English editorial tradition in which the parenthesis is normally reproduced, the three textbook national self-panegyrics used to illustrate the endless patriotic self-congratulation are the canonical Greek–English–German triad:

* Greek – the Pindaric topos *es cosa grande ser griego / a great thing is to be a Greek* – Pindar;
* English – the Victorian Whig patriotic panegyric of England in Macaulay’s *History of England* – Lord Thomas Babington Macaulay;
* German – the nationalist panegyric *Reden an die deutsche Nation* – Johann Gottlieb Fichte.

Pindar, Thomas Babington Macaulay, Johann Gottlieb Fichte

